/* ==============================================================
   init — put the robot in a known state
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   PURPOSE   Call this at the start of every program, so nothing
             depends on what the previous program left behind
   SAVE AS   00_common/01_init      (on the robot, via the MecaPortal)
   CALL      StartProgram("00_common/01_init")
   NEEDS     Robot activated (see 01_basic/01)
   MOVES     Nothing. Settings only.
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

   Almost every setting on this robot is STICKY. Set a speed, a
   tool frame or a blending value, and it stays that way for
   every command that follows — including the commands of the
   next program someone runs.

   So a program that does not set what it depends on is not
   really a program. It is a program plus an assumption about
   whatever ran before it. That works on the bench and produces
   the worst class of bug in a cell: the one where a sequence
   behaves differently depending on what was run first, and the
   engineer cannot reproduce it.

   One line at the top of every program removes that entirely:

       StartProgram("00_common/01_init")

   THE PART THAT CATCHES PEOPLE OUT

   Many of these settings DO reset to their factory defaults —
   but only when the robot is activated or reactivated. A robot
   that stays activated all day, running program after program,
   never gets that reset. Between two runs of your program, the
   only thing that resets these settings is you.

   Being explicit costs nothing and is worth doing even where it
   duplicates the default, because "the default" is a fact about
   the firmware version, not about your cell.

   HOW TO USE IT

   1. Open this file in the MecaPortal code editor.
   2. Change the values below to suit your cell — particularly
      SetTrf, which is wrong for every real tool.
   3. Save it on the robot as "00_common/01_init". The slash
      creates the folder. Names are case sensitive, up to 63
      characters, letters, digits and underscores.
   4. Put StartProgram("00_common/01_init") at the top of every
      program.

   WHY THIS FILE HAS NO DEACTIVATE/REACTIVATE BLOCK

   Every example in this repo opens with a deactivate/reactivate
   reset. This file deliberately does not, and must not.

   It is called BY other programs, with StartProgram, after they
   have already reset and reactivated. A deactivate in here would
   tear down the robot state its caller just established, and
   would wipe the settings this very file is about to apply.
   Helpers that run inside another program's flow do not reset;
   they configure.

   WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE

   Several settings cannot go in any running program, because the
   robot only accepts them while DEACTIVATED:

       SetWorkZoneLimits(...)     SetToolSphere(...)
       SetWorkZoneCfg(...)        SetCollisionCfg(...)
       SetJointLimits(...)        SetJointLimitsCfg(...)
       SetPStop2Cfg(...)

   That is by design — a running program must not be able to
   widen its own safety envelope. They are persistent and survive
   a power cycle, so set them once at commissioning. See
   03_advanced/04_work_zone_and_collision.mxprog.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============ 1. REFERENCE FRAMES ============================ */

/* WRF — where the world is measured from. Zeros put it back on
   the robot's own base (the BRF), which is the right neutral
   starting point. A program that works in a fixture frame should
   set its own SetWrf AFTER calling this file.
   See 02_intermediate/01_world_reference_frame.mxprog. */
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0)

/* TRF — where the tool tip is, measured from the flange (the
   lower end of the spline shaft). Zeros put it back on the end
   of the shaft.

   ⚠ THIS IS THE LINE TO CHANGE. Zeros are correct only for a
   bare shaft. With any real tool fitted, every pose in every
   program is wrong until this is right. Measure from the end of
   the shaft to the tool's working point — and note the z is
   NEGATIVE: every frame on this robot points up, and the tool
   hangs below the flange. SetTrf(0, 0, -30, 0) is a tool tip
   30 mm below the end of the shaft.
   See 02_intermediate/02_tool_reference_frame.mxprog. */
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ============ 2. POSTURE CONFIGURATION ======================= */

/* Let the robot pick the arm posture (lefty or righty) and the
   joint-4 revolution closest to where it already is. This is the
   safe general default and keeps motion small.

   In a real cell, PIN THESE INSTEAD. The manual is unusually
   direct about it: it strongly recommends always specifying the
   configuration. Automatic selection is what produces the
   occasional cycle that takes a surprising route, because the
   choice depends on where the robot happened to start. Comment
   out the two lines below and uncomment SetConf and SetConfTurn
   to make every cycle identical.
   See 02_intermediate/04 and 02_intermediate/05. */
SetAutoConf(1)
SetAutoConfTurn(1)

// SetConf(1)               // pin the arm posture: righty
// SetConfTurn(0)           // pin the joint-4 revolution


/* ============ 3. SPEEDS AND ACCELERATIONS ==================== */

/* Joint-space moves — MoveJoints, MovePose, MoveJump.
   Percentages of each joint's top rated speed. Firmware default
   is 25% velocity and 100% acceleration; we set both explicitly
   and slower, because this repo is demo material and an MCS500
   at full speed is genuinely fast. Raise SetJointVel for
   production cycle times.

   On the MCS500, SetJointAcc makes the robot come to a stop,
   even with blending on. Harmless here at the top of a program,
   worth remembering anywhere else. */
SetJointVel(20)
SetJointAcc(100)

/* The hard ceiling on every joint, for every kind of move. The
   default is 100, and a previous program — 03_advanced/07, for
   one — may have lowered it and been stopped before it put it
   back. */
SetJointVelLimit(100)

/* Cartesian moves — MoveLin and the relative variants. Real
   units: mm/s and deg/s. Firmware defaults are 150 mm/s and
   45 deg/s. 45 deg/s makes a straight line that also turns the
   tool noticeably slow, so the house value is 90. Set both,
   because a move that translates and turns at once is governed
   by whichever is binding.
   See 01_basic/06_linear_motion_settings.mxprog. */
SetCartLinVel(150)
SetCartAngVel(90)

/* SetCartAcc is deliberately left alone. Using it forces the
   robot to a complete stop between moves EVEN WHEN BLENDING IS
   ENABLED, which quietly undoes the blending section below. Set
   it only in the specific program that needs it. */
// SetCartAcc(50)

/* Velocity-based mode: the speeds above govern, and durations
   fall out of them. The alternative is time-based mode, where
   you specify how long each move should take — and in which
   MoveJump must not be used.
   See 03_advanced/06_time_based_motion.mxprog. */
SetMoveMode(0)


/* ============ 4. MOVEJUMP ==================================== */

/* The shape of the SCARA's pick-and-place move: how far it lifts
   straight up, how high the hop may go, and how gently it lands.
   These are the firmware defaults, stated explicitly because a
   previous program may have raised them to clear an obstacle
   that is not in your cell.
   See 02_intermediate/07_move_jump.mxprog. */
SetMoveJumpHeight(10, 10, 0, 102)
SetMoveJumpApproachVel(10, 1, 10, 1)


/* ============ 5. BLENDING ==================================== */

/* Round the corners between moves rather than stopping at every
   waypoint. On by default at 100%, but set explicitly because a
   previous program may well have turned it off at a work point
   and not turned it back on.

   Individual programs should still drop to SetBlending(0) around
   points where the tool actually has to arrive — and before any
   MoveJump that follows another MoveJump or a MovePose.
   See 02_intermediate/06_blending.mxprog. */
SetBlending(100)


/* ============ 6. PAYLOAD ===================================== */

/* What is hanging off the flange, in kg, and where its centre of
   mass sits, in mm from the flange. Below the flange is negative
   z on this robot.

   Zero means "bare shaft". If a tool is fitted, put its real
   mass here — this is the cheapest accuracy improvement
   available and it is routinely skipped. Programs that pick up a
   part should raise it while carrying and drop it again after
   placing.
   See 03_advanced/05_payload_and_torque_limits.mxprog. */
SetPayload(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ============ 7. TORQUE LIMITS =============================== */

/* Release any torque limits a previous program tightened, so
   ordinary motion is not tripping a guard meant for one delicate
   placement. 100% on every joint is the firmware default and
   nothing will realistically trip against it.

   The config below restores the firmware default too: severity 0
   means NO ACTION on a trip, and mode 2 is deviation-from-
   predicted detection. Note that there is no "detection off"
   mode — every mode value is an active detection method, so it
   is the SEVERITY that decides whether a trip does anything.
   A program that wants torque guarding sets both, for the few
   millimetres where contact is plausible, then releases them. */
SetTorqueLimits(100, 100, 100, 100)
SetTorqueLimitsCfg(0, 2)


/* ============ 8. MARKER ====================================== */

/* A line in the robot log saying initialisation finished. When
   you are reading a log after the fact, knowing exactly where
   each cycle began is worth the one command. */
LogTrace("00_common/01_init complete")

/* A checkpoint the host can wait on, so a PLC or Python program
   knows initialisation is done before it issues real work.
   Number 100 is reserved for this across the repo — keep your
   program's own checkpoints below it.
   See 01_basic/07_delay_and_sequencing.mxprog. */
SetCheckpoint(100)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   A NOTE ON CALLING THIS FROM ANOTHER PROGRAM

   StartProgram runs a program saved in the robot's memory, once.
   If it is not saved, you get [3017], no offline program saved —
   so save this file before putting the call anywhere.

   The settings above are motion commands, so they enter the
   motion queue in order. Placing StartProgram("00_common/01_init")
   at the TOP of a program therefore means these settings are
   applied before that program's own moves, which is exactly what
   you want. A program that needs its own frame or speed simply
   sets it after the call, overriding what is here.

   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run 02_intermediate/06_blending.mxprog and stop it part-way
      through, while blending is off. Then run a pick-and-place
      and watch it behave differently than expected. Then add
      StartProgram("00_common/01_init") at the top and run again.
      This is a concrete, thirty-second demonstration of why
      cells become flaky, and how one line fixes it.

   2. For an integrator, the argument is repeatability: every
      cycle starts from an identical, explicitly declared state,
      and that state is under version control in one file rather
      than scattered across twenty programs.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
